# A causal loop: CTA c runs c trips, so the count is %ctaid.x and the
# guard skipping CTA 0 is the loop's entry. Per CTA the totals are
# exact in %ctaid.x; over the launch, where which threads run the loop
# varies by CTA, they are unknown, where on an RTX 4090 with 4 CTAs the
# barrier runs 192 times. Without the guard CTA 0 runs once, the count
# is max(%ctaid.x, 1), and its sum over the grid, 7, gives the 224
# barriers the RTX 4090 runs (micro/cta_trips.unguarded.ncu.json).
RUN: %ptxroof analyze %fixtures/micro/cta_trips.ptx | FileCheck "%s"
RUN: %ptxroof analyze %fixtures/micro/cta_trips.ptx --ncu %fixtures/micro/cta_trips.ncu.json | FileCheck --check-prefix=REC "%s"
RUN: %ptxroof analyze %fixtures/micro/cta_trips.ptx --ncu %fixtures/micro/cta_trips.unguarded.ncu.json | FileCheck --check-prefix=SUM "%s"
CHECK: trips = %ctaid.x
CHECK: totals per CTA [static]:
CHECK: bar.sync{{ +}}32 * %ctaid.x{{ +}}%ctaid.x{{$}}
REC: totals per launch [static]:
REC: bar.sync{{ +}}0 + unknown{{ +}}0 + unknown{{$}}
REC: $L__BB0_2  unchecked
SUM-LABEL: kernel micro_cta_trips_unguarded
SUM: trips = max(%ctaid.x, 1)
SUM: totals per launch [static]:
SUM: bar.sync{{ +}}224{{ +}}7{{$}}
SUM: $L__BB1_1  confirmed
SUM: measured L2 sectors of global accesses: 1 lines agree (1 exact, 0 within a range, 0 within a bound), 0 differ
